Two properties, and they are not the same one:

- Everything must be possible without pidly installed. A checkout handed to
  someone else is navigable with find, sed, mv and an editor. This is
  principle 1 and it is already true.
- Nothing should require it. With the package installed, a human or an agent
  should never have to reach past the CLI to get ordinary work done. This is
  not true today and nobody has ever listed the gaps.

The test to apply: any bash a human or an agent writes to interact with
tickets is a missing command. Not "could pidly do this" but "did someone
have to write a loop".

The evidence is b5444d3..373a546, nineteen commits on 2026-09-16 and
2026-09-17 that implemented 4, 10, 11, 12, 17 and 22, closed 2, and opened
23 through 40. Everything below was needed during that work and has no
command today. The operation is the fact; the suggested name is not.

  operation                     what it takes today
  --------------------------------------------------------------------
  resolve a number to a path    find .pidly -type d -name 17
  read one ticket               cat "$(find .pidly -type d -name 11)/info"
  read one field, one ticket    sed -n 's/^created: //p' <path>/info
  read one field, many tickets  a for loop around the above (27 covers this)
  read the field block          awk 'NR>3 { if ($0=="") exit; print }'
  set a field on a new ticket   sed -i '3a effort: M\narea: cli'
  retitle                       sed -i '3s/.*/title: .../'
  add or drop a dependency      sed -i '2s/.*/needs: 24/'
  number, claim and title       find .pidly -name info | while read f, then
    in one listing               sed -n 1p and sed -n 3p per file
  re-parent a ticket            mv, with nothing checking where it lands
  what closed, or closed last   find, or git log
  search bodies                 grep -r, and knowing to exclude c/
  anything in bulk              a shell loop
  change part of a body         extract it with awk, transform it, and pipe
                                 the whole body back through pidly edit -b

Two of those are worth singling out. Setting a field on a ticket new just
made happened for every one of the eighteen tickets opened in that range —
it is the most repeated manual step in the tracker's history, and 24 and 25
are why. And resolving a number to a path was needed to do almost anything
else, including by the audit's own commands above.

The last row is the worst of them, because the workaround is more dangerous
than the gap. edit writes a body three ways: an editor, which an agent
cannot drive; -a, which appends; and -b, which replaces the whole thing. So
changing one paragraph non-interactively means reconstructing the entire
body and piping it back, and a mistake in the reconstruction silently
deletes whatever was left out. That is how 24 and 28 were corrected in
99ebca6 — extract with awk, replace a passage in a temp file, pipe it all
back — and it only survived review because the tickets were short enough to
verify by eye. The commands that would remove it are the same ones that
would remove several rows above: something that edits a field or a passage
in place rather than a whole file.

The pattern to aim for: mkdir -p .pidly/c && mv .pidly/2 .pidly/c/ appears
throughout the history until b5444d3, which added close and reopen, and
never again. That is what closing a gap looks like. Every other line above
is still the only way.

The boundary, because it will be tested by the first request that does not
fit: "what is the average effort across my open tickets" is 27 plus
arithmetic. One command hands over every effort value with its ticket
number, and the caller does the rest. pidly hands over data in one pass; it
does not compute statistics and it does not grow a query language. Not on
purity grounds — a tally is one pipeline over well-formed output, and every
aggregate pidly owned would be one somebody wanted differently.

The wrapper stays thin for the same reason. Each of these is a command over
the format, not an abstraction on top of it, and every one must do exactly
what the hand idiom in the reference does, with the reference still
documenting that idiom beside it. A command that quietly becomes the only
way to do something satisfies the second property by breaking the first.

Three outcomes per item, and the third is a real answer: a command exists, a
command should exist and here is its ticket, or this stays manual on
purpose. Deleting a ticket is the model for the third — deliberately not a
command, and never will be.

To redo this audit later rather than trust this list: work in the tracker
for a day and write down every command that does not begin with pidly.

Done when every operation in the reference and every row above has one of
the three answers, the gaps worth closing are child tickets of this one, and
the reference and the command set agree with each other.
